  "image_name": "41560_2025_1937_Fig1_HTML.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares interfacial solvation structures near a negatively charged electrode in three electrolyte regimes and shows how changing concentration alters the interfacial solid-state layer (ISS/SEI). In a conventional electrolyte, lithium ions are mainly surrounded by solvent molecules, while anions and additives are less involved at the interface. This condition is linked to good bulk properties such as high ionic conductivity and low cost, but the interfacial layer appears more solvent-dominated.\n\nAs concentration increases, the solvation shell changes through stronger coordination, ion pairing, and aggregation. In the super-concentrated case, the ISS becomes anion-rich, with lithium coordinated more often by anions and clustered species near the electrode. The right side then presents a moderately concentrated electrolyte, around 1.0 M, where both anions and additives are enriched at the interface. This seems to combine desirable interfacial chemistry with less extreme concentration.\n\nThe overall message is that tuning electrolyte concentration reshapes the local lithium solvation environment at the electrode, which in turn changes the composition and quality of the interphase. The moderately concentrated, anion- and additive-rich interface is highlighted as producing an SEI that is thin, robust, and highly ionically conductive.",
